{"name":"Metallic Blue","hex":"#4f738e","fetched":"2026-10-12","looked":{"definitions":[{"term":"metallic","whole":false,"parts":[{"part":"Adjective","definitions":["Of, relating to, or characteristic of metal.","Made of or containing metal."]},{"part":"Noun","definitions":["A metallic color."]}],"link":"https://en.wiktionary.org/wiki/metallic"},{"term":"blue","whole":false,"parts":[{"part":"Adjective","definitions":["Of a blue hue.","Depressed, melancholic, sad."]},{"part":"Noun","definitions":["The colour of the clear sky or the deep sea; the colour midway between green and violet in the visible spectrum and one of the primary additive colours.","Anything coloured blue, especially to distinguish it from similar objects differing only in colour."]}],"link":"https://en.wiktionary.org/wiki/blue"}],"entries":[{"title":"Metallic color","blocks":[{"text":"A metallic color is a color that appears to be that of a polished metal. The visual sensation usually associated with metals is its metallic shine. This cannot be reproduced by a simple solid color, because the shiny effect is due to the material's brightness varying with the surface angle to the light source. In addition, there is no mechanism for showing metallic or fluorescent colors on a computer without resorting to rendering software which simulates the action of light on a shiny surface. Consequently in art and in heraldry one would normally use a metallic paint that glitters like a real metal."}],"image":"https://thumb.wikimedia.org/wikipedia/commons/thumb/5/55/Silver_crystal.jpg/330px-Silver_crystal.jpg?utm_source=en.wikipedia.org&utm_campaign=api&utm_content=thumbnail","link":"https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/Metallic_color","wikidata":"Q25312687"},{"title":"Blue","blocks":[{"text":"Blue is one of the three primary colours in the RGB (additive) colour model, as well as in the RYB colour model. It lies between violet and cyan on the spectrum of visible light. The term blue generally describes colours perceived by humans observing light with a dominant wavelength between approximately 450 and 495 nanometres. The clear daytime sky and the deep sea appear blue because of an optical effect known as Rayleigh scattering. An optical effect called the Tyndall effect explains blue eyes. Distant objects appear more blue because of another optical effect called aerial perspective."}],"image":"https://thumb.wikimedia.org/wikipedia/commons/thumb/5/5a/Earth-in-true-color-himawari-09.jpg/330px-Earth-in-true-color-himawari-09.jpg?utm_source=en.wikipedia.org&utm_campaign=api&utm_content=thumbnail","link":"https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/Blue","wikidata":"Q1088"}],"images":[{"srcset":"https://thumb.wikimedia.org/wikipedia/commons/thumb/6/68/Platinum_crystals.jpg/500px-Platinum_crystals.jpg?utm_source=en.wikipedia.org&utm_campaign=parser&utm_content=thumbnail 1x, https://thumb.wikimedia.org/wikipedia/commons/thumb/6/68/Platinum_crystals.jpg/1280px-Platinum_crystals.jpg?utm_source=en.wikipedia.org&utm_campaign=parser&utm_content=thumbnail 2x","src":"https://thumb.wikimedia.org/wikipedia/commons/thumb/6/68/Platinum_crystals.jpg/500px-Platinum_crystals.jpg?utm_source=en.wikipedia.org&utm_campaign=parser&utm_content=thumbnail","alt":"Platinum crystals","link":"https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/File%3APlatinum_crystals.jpg"},{"srcset":"https://thumb.wikimedia.org/wikipedia/commons/thumb/6/64/Austrolestes_cingulatus_male.jpg/500px-Austrolestes_cingulatus_male.jpg?utm_source=en.wikipedia.org&utm_campaign=parser&utm_content=thumbnail 1x, https://thumb.wikimedia.org/wikipedia/commons/thumb/6/64/Austrolestes_cingulatus_male.jpg/1280px-Austrolestes_cingulatus_male.jpg?utm_source=en.wikipedia.org&utm_campaign=parser&utm_content=thumbnail 2x","src":"https://thumb.wikimedia.org/wikipedia/commons/thumb/6/64/Austrolestes_cingulatus_male.jpg/500px-Austrolestes_cingulatus_male.jpg?utm_source=en.wikipedia.org&utm_campaign=parser&utm_content=thumbnail","alt":"The damselfly Austrolestes cingulatus (the metallic ringtail) is noted for its naturally occurring metallic color. Additionally, such insect coloring often leads to circular polarization of reflected light.","link":"https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/File%3AAustrolestes_cingulatus_male.jpg"},{"srcset":"https://thumb.wikimedia.org/wikipedia/commons/thumb/8/89/BLUE_METALLIC_TEXTURE_%287241672566%29.jpg/120px-BLUE_METALLIC_TEXTURE_%287241672566%29.jpg?utm_source=en.wikipedia.org&utm_campaign=parser&utm_content=thumbnail 1x, https://thumb.wikimedia.org/wikipedia/commons/thumb/8/89/BLUE_METALLIC_TEXTURE_%287241672566%29.jpg/250px-BLUE_METALLIC_TEXTURE_%287241672566%29.jpg?utm_source=en.wikipedia.org&utm_campaign=parser&utm_content=thumbnail 2x","src":"https://thumb.wikimedia.org/wikipedia/commons/thumb/8/89/BLUE_METALLIC_TEXTURE_%287241672566%29.jpg/120px-BLUE_METALLIC_TEXTURE_%287241672566%29.jpg?utm_source=en.wikipedia.org&utm_campaign=parser&utm_content=thumbnail","alt":"","link":"https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/File%3ABLUE_METALLIC_TEXTURE_(7241672566).jpg"},{"srcset":"https://thumb.wikimedia.org/wikipedia/commons/thumb/d/d8/GOLD_METALLIC_TEXTURE_%287241678418%29.jpg/120px-GOLD_METALLIC_TEXTURE_%287241678418%29.jpg?utm_source=en.wikipedia.org&utm_campaign=parser&utm_content=thumbnail 1x, https://thumb.wikimedia.org/wikipedia/commons/thumb/d/d8/GOLD_METALLIC_TEXTURE_%287241678418%29.jpg/250px-GOLD_METALLIC_TEXTURE_%287241678418%29.jpg?utm_source=en.wikipedia.org&utm_campaign=parser&utm_content=thumbnail 2x","src":"https://thumb.wikimedia.org/wikipedia/commons/thumb/d/d8/GOLD_METALLIC_TEXTURE_%287241678418%29.jpg/120px-GOLD_METALLIC_TEXTURE_%287241678418%29.jpg?utm_source=en.wikipedia.org&utm_campaign=parser&utm_content=thumbnail","alt":"","link":"https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/File%3AGOLD_METALLIC_TEXTURE_(7241678418).jpg"},{"srcset":"https://thumb.wikimedia.org/wikipedia/commons/thumb/d/d6/Green_METALLIC_TEXTURE_%287241685310%29.jpg/120px-Green_METALLIC_TEXTURE_%287241685310%29.jpg?utm_source=en.wikipedia.org&utm_campaign=parser&utm